1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,286
Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:02,836 --> 00:00:06,924
My name is Hieu Nguyen Trung, and I teach at FPT University,

3
00:00:06,924 --> 00:00:08,560
Ho Chi Minh City Campus.

4
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:11,888
Today I will present a methodological paper:

5
00:00:11,888 --> 00:00:14,665
From Provenance to Interpretation Readiness.

6
00:00:14,665 --> 00:00:19,527
A Methodological Framework for Cross-Lingual Audits of Large Language Models.

7
00:00:21,277 --> 00:00:25,903
The question behind it is simple. When a model answers differently in two languages,

8
00:00:25,903 --> 00:00:27,885
what exactly are we entitled to say?

9
00:00:30,435 --> 00:00:32,235
I will move in five steps.

10
00:00:32,785 --> 00:00:37,234
First, the attribution problem. Second, two English–Vietnamese cases.

11
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:41,463
Third, the framework and its five provenance layers. Fourth,

12
00:00:41,463 --> 00:00:43,731
what I call interpretation readiness.

13
00:00:44,281 --> 00:00:47,462
And finally, the scope of the paper and how it should be validated.

14
00:00:50,012 --> 00:00:54,970
Many audits now ask large language models political or value-laden questions in

15
00:00:54,970 --> 00:00:57,605
several languages and compare the answers.

16
00:00:58,155 --> 00:01:02,137
The tempting reading is that the model holds different values in different languages.

17
00:01:03,887 --> 00:01:06,523
But a language switch is not one variable.

18
00:01:07,073 --> 00:01:10,364
It changes the item itself: the wording, the register,

19
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:12,437
the institutions a term refers to.

20
00:01:12,987 --> 00:01:16,646
It can change model behaviour: how well the model understands,

21
00:01:16,646 --> 00:01:19,360
how it uses a response scale, when it refuses.

22
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:24,105
And it can change the procedure: the response format, the model access,

23
00:01:24,105 --> 00:01:25,936
the way the calls are executed.

24
00:01:27,686 --> 00:01:29,506
So my research question is this:

25
00:01:29,506 --> 00:01:33,944
when may an observed cross-lingual difference count as evidence about a model?

26
00:01:34,494 --> 00:01:38,070
Scores alone cannot separate these components after the fact.

27
00:01:38,620 --> 00:01:41,796
If we simply call the leftover difference "localisation",

28
00:01:41,796 --> 00:01:43,635
we replace evidence with a story.

29
00:01:46,185 --> 00:01:49,970
Let me be clear about what is not missing. Coverage is no longer the gap.

30
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:55,191
Helwe and colleagues evaluate fifteen models across thirty-three languages,

31
00:01:55,191 --> 00:01:56,499
including Vietnamese.

32
00:01:57,049 --> 00:02:02,715
Shankar and colleagues include Vietnamese in an audit of Asian public opinion.

33
00:02:03,265 --> 00:02:09,065
Labat and colleagues use human-translated European Values Study items in eight languages,

34
00:02:09,065 --> 00:02:14,213
and Lim and Röttger build thirty-six thousand parallel English–Chinese prompts.

35
00:02:15,963 --> 00:02:21,068
What varies is how equivalence is handled. It is inherited from a survey, asserted,

36
00:02:21,068 --> 00:02:23,036
or verified one link downstream.

37
00:02:23,586 --> 00:02:28,242
And downstream review cannot reconstruct an upstream step that nobody recorded.

38
00:02:29,992 --> 00:02:35,692
So the gap is a shared account of which records and which diagnostics an exact claim requires.

39
00:02:38,242 --> 00:02:43,093
To make this concrete, I use two constructed English–Vietnamese diagnostics.

40
00:02:43,093 --> 00:02:46,093
They are illustrations, not model observations.

41
00:02:47,843 --> 00:02:49,616
The first is a negative control.

42
00:02:49,616 --> 00:02:54,603
The English phrase state–market coordination describes a relation that can vary by degree.

43
00:02:55,153 --> 00:03:00,504
Its direct Vietnamese rendering is phối hợp giữa nhà nước và thị trường.

44
00:03:02,254 --> 00:03:08,290
But a different Vietnamese phrase, kinh tế thị trường định hướng xã hội chủ nghĩa,

45
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:12,191
back-translates as socialist-oriented market economy.

46
00:03:12,741 --> 00:03:16,723
That names a political-economic model, not a degree of coordination.

47
00:03:18,473 --> 00:03:20,541
If an audit swapped one for the other,

48
00:03:20,541 --> 00:03:25,602
an item about "more or less coordination" would become an item about endorsing a named model.

49
00:03:26,152 --> 00:03:29,879
Keeping the same five-point scale would not restore comparability.

50
00:03:30,429 --> 00:03:33,401
This is construct adaptation, not translation.

51
00:03:33,951 --> 00:03:38,827
A good provenance record should make that substitution visible before any model is queried.

52
00:03:41,377 --> 00:03:43,607
The second case is subtler.

53
00:03:43,607 --> 00:03:48,646
Civil society and xã hội dân sự are a plausible lexical pair.

54
00:03:49,196 --> 00:03:55,172
But a policy question about handing services to civil-society organisations is underspecified:

55
00:03:55,172 --> 00:03:58,159
which organisations, and in which jurisdiction?

56
00:03:58,709 --> 00:04:04,340
The prompt language may lead a model to resolve the same label against different institutional settings.

57
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:09,022
I do not claim the two terms denote different things.

58
00:04:09,022 --> 00:04:13,117
I claim this is a material alternative explanation, and it can be bounded.

59
00:04:13,667 --> 00:04:15,996
One diagnostic is an anchored item:

60
00:04:15,996 --> 00:04:19,524
the same definition and actor list in both languages,

61
00:04:19,524 --> 00:04:21,654
randomised against the original.

62
00:04:22,204 --> 00:04:23,984
Another is a competence probe:

63
00:04:23,984 --> 00:04:27,603
do both languages classify the listed actors in the same way?

64
00:04:29,353 --> 00:04:30,674
Without such evidence,

65
00:04:30,674 --> 00:04:34,396
the admissible claim stays a between-language-form divergence,

66
00:04:34,396 --> 00:04:36,017
not a normative difference.

67
00:04:38,567 --> 00:04:44,074
The framework I propose is the Provenance-Controlled Cross-Lingual Audit Framework,

68
00:04:44,074 --> 00:04:44,604
or PCAF.

69
00:04:45,154 --> 00:04:48,280
An audit result is produced by a chain of transformations,

70
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:51,459
and each layer names one of them and what must be recorded.

71
00:04:53,209 --> 00:04:57,481
Layer one is the prompt: the source of the item and the full realised prompt.

72
00:04:58,031 --> 00:05:02,525
Layer two is translation: the method or translator, the direction,

73
00:05:02,525 --> 00:05:06,611
the type of transformation, and how equivalence was checked.

74
00:05:07,161 --> 00:05:11,857
Layer three is the model: the most specific identifier, the endpoint,

75
00:05:11,857 --> 00:05:13,082
the access window.

76
00:05:13,632 --> 00:05:18,981
Layer four is execution: decoding, repeats, randomisation, parsing, and,

77
00:05:18,981 --> 00:05:23,512
importantly, refusals and malformed outputs kept as outcomes.

78
00:05:25,262 --> 00:05:26,686
Layer five is different.

79
00:05:26,686 --> 00:05:31,847
It is the retained artefacts that let someone else inspect or recompute the first four.

80
00:05:34,397 --> 00:05:39,367
Formally, an observed answer in a language is the result of composing these steps,

81
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:41,851
from item specification to parsed output.

82
00:05:42,401 --> 00:05:47,254
An evidence transformation is any step that can change the datum or the task.

83
00:05:49,004 --> 00:05:50,726
Provenance has two dimensions.

84
00:05:50,726 --> 00:05:55,833
Conceptual provenance covers what construct was specified and what happened in rendering.

85
00:05:56,383 --> 00:06:00,985
Access provenance covers which model was queried, when, and under what regime.

86
00:06:00,985 --> 00:06:03,108
One cannot substitute for the other.

87
00:06:04,858 --> 00:06:09,781
And provenance is not validity. A fully recorded step may still be the wrong step.

88
00:06:10,331 --> 00:06:12,343
Records make the path inspectable;

89
00:06:12,343 --> 00:06:16,426
diagnostics decide whether a transformation could explain the result.

90
00:06:18,976 --> 00:06:22,645
This brings me to the central idea: interpretation readiness.

91
00:06:23,195 --> 00:06:28,802
A result is interpretation-ready for a specific claim C when three things hold.

92
00:06:29,352 --> 00:06:31,756
The records needed for C can be audited.

93
00:06:32,306 --> 00:06:37,762
Each material alternative is either excluded or bounded tightly enough to preserve C.

94
00:06:38,312 --> 00:06:41,006
And the remaining uncertainty is stated in the claim.

95
00:06:42,756 --> 00:06:49,524
If the evidence supports only a narrower claim C′ that narrower claim must be reassessed in its own right.

96
00:06:51,274 --> 00:06:53,549
What counts as a material alternative?

97
00:06:53,549 --> 00:06:57,321
A design-linked mechanism that could change the sign, the size,

98
00:06:57,321 --> 00:06:59,776
the ordering or the meaning of the claim.

99
00:07:00,326 --> 00:07:04,970
For example: filtering out invalid answers changes who is being compared.

100
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:08,654
An open institutional referent means the tasks may differ.

101
00:07:09,204 --> 00:07:13,210
And version drift matters when language blocks are run at different times.

102
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:18,952
PCAF distinguishes four families of claims.

103
00:07:19,502 --> 00:07:24,007
O is recomputable observation: the retained outputs say what they say.

104
00:07:24,557 --> 00:07:26,181
R is repeated divergence:

105
00:07:26,181 --> 00:07:30,664
the difference recurs under a documented access and execution regime.

106
00:07:31,214 --> 00:07:36,568
N is language-conditioned normative variation, the strongest interpretive claim,

107
00:07:36,568 --> 00:07:40,316
which also needs equivalence and competence diagnostics.

108
00:07:40,866 --> 00:07:45,591
And E is resemblance to an external reference, such as a survey population.

109
00:07:47,341 --> 00:07:49,141
The important point is the shape.

110
00:07:50,891 --> 00:07:54,630
These are not four cumulative rungs. E branches from observation;

111
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:58,138
it does not require a normative difference between languages.

112
00:07:58,688 --> 00:08:02,380
Two languages can be equal and both resemble the same reference.

113
00:08:02,930 --> 00:08:08,399
The dashed arrow says that only a repeated resemblance claim also needs the evidence of R.

114
00:08:08,949 --> 00:08:12,490
The arrows are evidence requirements, not causal effects.

115
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:17,594
Here is how this changes wording in practice.

116
00:08:18,144 --> 00:08:22,131
Helwe and colleagues aggregate valid answers and exclude false labels;

117
00:08:22,131 --> 00:08:24,239
this is reported in their Appendix B.

118
00:08:24,789 --> 00:08:29,851
A broad claim would be about normative differences across the full response distribution.

119
00:08:31,601 --> 00:08:36,452
But invalid-output rates can differ by language, which changes the conditioning set.

120
00:08:36,452 --> 00:08:38,242
That is a material alternative.

121
00:08:38,792 --> 00:08:43,622
The defensible, narrower claim C′ is about the retained valid answers.

122
00:08:44,172 --> 00:08:47,817
It reaches O, and R if the model and execution records suffice.

123
00:08:49,567 --> 00:08:53,166
It does not reach N: translation equivalence is not addressed,

124
00:08:53,166 --> 00:08:57,520
and keeping only valid answers may select different items in each language.

125
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:02,965
I want to stress what this is not. It is a change in admissible wording.

126
00:09:03,515 --> 00:09:08,019
It is not a recomputed effect, and it is not a quality rating of that study.

127
00:09:10,569 --> 00:09:14,531
Let me be explicit about the limits of this paper. No model was queried.

128
00:09:15,081 --> 00:09:18,808
The English–Vietnamese cases are constructed diagnostics.

129
00:09:19,358 --> 00:09:23,086
The four audits were selected purposively and read by one author,

130
00:09:23,086 --> 00:09:26,126
so there is no prevalence claim and no quality score.

131
00:09:26,676 --> 00:09:29,559
And PCAF evaluates the support for a claim;

132
00:09:29,559 --> 00:09:32,577
it does not judge a model's bias or ideology,

133
00:09:32,577 --> 00:09:35,326
and it is not a certification instrument.

134
00:09:37,076 --> 00:09:39,311
Validation is therefore the next step,

135
00:09:39,311 --> 00:09:43,252
and the paper proposes two studies that have not yet been executed.

136
00:09:43,802 --> 00:09:49,062
One is an independent coding study: at least two reviewers, a frozen codebook,

137
00:09:49,062 --> 00:09:52,568
a two-stage protocol, and chance-adjusted agreement.

138
00:09:53,118 --> 00:09:57,877
The other is a prospective model pilot: paired items, randomised order,

139
00:09:57,877 --> 00:10:02,301
all raw outcomes retained, including refusals, and costs measured.

140
00:10:02,851 --> 00:10:07,275
Only these studies can show whether the framework is reliable and useful.

141
00:10:09,825 --> 00:10:10,695
To conclude.

142
00:10:12,445 --> 00:10:14,872
Language coverage does not settle attribution.

143
00:10:15,422 --> 00:10:20,417
PCAF separates the provenance of evidence transformations from diagnostics,

144
00:10:20,417 --> 00:10:24,013
and both from claim-specific interpretation readiness.

145
00:10:25,763 --> 00:10:27,411
The practical rule is simple:

146
00:10:27,411 --> 00:10:31,105
make unresolved alternatives visible in the wording of the claim,

147
00:10:31,105 --> 00:10:34,970
instead of turning documentation completeness into a validity score.

148
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:40,807
The codebook and a blank audit register are openly available on Zenodo,

149
00:10:40,807 --> 00:10:42,246
at the link on the slide.

150
00:10:42,796 --> 00:10:46,328
Thank you very much for your attention. I am happy to take your questions.
